  "ecological_result": {
    "supported": "Annual plant-pollinator link dynamics carry a reproducible current-state opportunity signal after the focal dyad response is excluded from predictor construction. The signal is dominated by plant flowering availability and pollinator activity on other plants; dyad-specific weekly overlap adds only a small, scope-sensitive increment.",
    "formation_read": "In the primary scope, a previously absent dyad with positive current-year focal-excluded opportunity forms a link about 3.61 times as often as a zero-opportunity dyad. This is a contemporaneous opportunity association, not a causal effect.",
    "persistence_read": "Current-year opportunity also discriminates link loss moderately, whereas the one-year-lagged surface performs worse than the null for loss.",
    "negative_control_read": "The same variables measured one year earlier do not recover the current-state signal: gain discrimination is near chance and loss prediction is worse than null. This argues against interpreting the result as a stable dyad propensity or a useful past-only forecasting rule.",
    "pairwise_matching_read": "The pair-specific overlap term is not the main ecological result: its incremental log-loss contribution is small and becomes fold-unstable in broader temporal sensitivities."
  },
